          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            is much like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            but possibly with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            of the form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+points"} fixed points
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 10px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OrbifoldTypes.jpg", :width => "240px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#HydeRamsdenRobins14"} Hyde-Ramsden-Robins 14
          %p#OrbifoldChartIdea
            Where a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            locally modeled on
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+spaces"} Euclidean spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %em orbifold
            is, more generally, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            that is locally modeled on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            of
            = succeed "s," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} actions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+points"} fixed points
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            become
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singular
            points in the quotient, like the tip of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 10px 10px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OrbifoldChartSchematics.png", :width => "440px"}/
          %p
            One way to make precise the nature of these quotients is as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+stack"} stacky
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotients"} homotopy quotients
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoids"} action groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n\sslash G
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MoerdijkPronk97"} Moerdijk &amp; Pronk 1997
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Moerdijk02"} Moerdijk 2002
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+groupoid"} proper
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+groupoid"} étale
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
            Lie groupoids understood to give the same orbifold this means that orbifolds are (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+groupoid"}> étale
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stacks"} smooth stacks
          %p
            The word
            %em orbifold
            was introduced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Thurston92"} Thurston 1992
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Satake1956"} Satake 1956
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              \-manifolds
            and took them in a more restrictive sense, assuming that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
            on the charts are always
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+group+action"} effective
            = succeed "." do
              %em effective orbifolds
          %blockquote
            %p
              There is also a notion of finite stabilizers in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
              %em orbifold
              if it has only so-called
              = succeed "." do
                %em orbifold singularities
          %p
            %br/
          %p#ArtisticIllustrations
            Artistic illustrations of 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+orbifolds"} flat orbifolds
            (arising as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+orbifold"} quotients
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
