          %h2#references References
          %h3#ReferencesGeneral General
          %p
            Essentially the modern definition of orbifolds
            %a{:href => "#DefinitionViaOrbifoldAtlases"} via orbifold atlases
            was first given by:
          %ul
            %li#Satake1956
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ichiro+Satake"}> Ichiro Satake
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On a generalisation of the notion of manifold
                %strong 42
                (1956), 359-363 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.42.6.359"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.42.6.359
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ichiro+Satake"}> Ichiro Satake
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    The Gauss-Bonnet theorem for
                    = succeed "-manifolds" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi V
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %strong 9
                (1957) 464-492 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jmsj/1261153826"}> euclid:1261153826
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            under the name “V-manifolds”. (Satake’s motivation for this terminology has not survived. One claim &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/97901/381"}> MO:q/97901
            \&amp;rbrack; is that: According to a colleague who asked Satake about it, the “V” in “V-manifold” stands for the German word ‘
            %em> Verzweigung
            , which means “branching” or “ramification”, as in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ramified+cover"} ramified cover
          %p The term “orbifold” was introduced by:
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:-30px 0px 00px 20px;"}
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Thurston-3dGeometry-2002.pdf#page=318"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Thurston-NamingOrbifold.png", :width => "500"}/
          %ul
            %li#Thurston92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Thurston"}> William Thurston
              , §3.2 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Three-dimensional geometry and topology
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/ThurstonTheGeometryAndTopologyOfThreeManifolds/mode/2up"} ark:/13960/t3714t34v
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Thurston-3dGeometry-1991.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.unl.edu/~jkettinger2/thurston.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Thurston-3dGeometry-2002.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p the first three chapters of which are published in expanded form as:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Thurston"}> William Thurston
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Geometry and Topology of Three-Manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691083049/three-dimensional-geometry-and-topology-volume-1"} ISBN:9780691083049
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_geometry_and_topology_of_three-manifolds"} Wikipedia page
          %p Further early discussion:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Haefliger"}> André Haefliger
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Groupoides d’holonomie et classifiants
                %strong 116
                (1984) 70-97 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/AST_1984__116__70_0/"}> numdam:AST_1984__116__70_0/
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scott"}> Peter Scott
                , section 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Geometries of 3-Manifolds
                %strong 15
                5 (1983) 401-487 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/blms/15.5.401"}> doi:10.1112/blms/15.5.401
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://hdl.handle.net/2027.42/135276"} hdl:2027.42/135276
          %p
            and specifically for orbifolds in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Lewis+Baily"}> Walter Lewis Baily
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the quotient of an analytic manifold by a group of analytic homeomorphisms
                %strong 40
                9 (1954) 804-808 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.40.9.804"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.40.9.804
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Lewis+Baily"}> Walter Lewis Baily
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Decomposition Theorem for V-Manifolds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2372472"} jstor:2372472
          %p
            For careful comparative review of the definitions in these original articles see also
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#IKZ10"} IKZ 10
          %p Survey of basic orbifold theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Daryl Cooper, Craig Hodgson, Steve Kerckhoff,
                = succeed "," do
